        <strong>Budget considerations:</strong> Starting prices range from {formatPrice(minPrc)}/mo to premium gigabit plans. The average entry price is ${avgPrice}/mo.
        {cheapest ? ` ${cheapest.name} has the most affordable starting plan.` : ''} Promotional pricing typically lasts 12–24 months.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Technology:</strong> {fiberProviders.length > 0 ? `Fiber internet from ${fiberProviders.slice(0, 2).map(p => p.name).join(' and ')} delivers symmetric speeds and minimal latency — ideal for video conferencing and gaming.` : 'Cable internet provides the fastest wired speeds in most areas.'}
        {fiveGProviders.length > 0 ? ` 5G home internet from ${fiveGProviders.slice(0, 2).map(p => p.name).join(' and ')} requires no wiring and typically has no contracts.` : ''}
      </p>

      <h3 style="margin-top:var(--space-lg);">Internet Availability Across {stateData.name}</h3>
      <p>
        {census ? `${formatPercent(census.urbanPct)} of ${stateData.name}'s population lives in urban areas with the most provider competition. ` : ''}
        Metro areas like {metroCities.slice(0, 3).map(c => c.name).join(', ')} typically offer 5+ providers including fiber.
        Rural areas may have fewer wired options, though satellite and fixed wireless extend coverage statewide.
      </p>
      <p>
        {census && census.underservedPct > 15
          ? `Approximately ${formatPercent(census.underservedPct)} of ${stateData.name} households are underserved (below 25/3 Mbps). Federal and state broadband expansion programs are actively deploying new infrastructure.`
          : census ? `${stateData.name} has relatively strong coverage — only ${formatPercent(census.underservedPct)} of households lack adequate broadband.` : 'Coverage continues to expand with ongoing infrastructure investment.'}
      </p>

      <h3 style="margin-top:var(--space-lg);">Saving Money on Internet in {stateData.name}</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>1. Compare all options.</strong> Use our free comparison tool — we regularly find savings of $20–50/mo.
        <strong>2. Watch for promo rates.</strong> Know the regular price before signing up.
        <strong>3. Buy your own equipment.</strong> Skip the $10–15/mo rental fee.
        <strong>4. Check low-income programs.</strong> FCC Lifeline, Spectrum Internet Assist, and AT&T Access can help qualifying households. <a href="/low-income-internet">See all programs</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
